<text layer="alpha" matrix="1 0 0 1 0 9.6" pin="h" transformations="translations" pos="0 483.203" stroke="black" type="minipage" width="920" height="19.3928" depth="5.404" valign="top">We consider the Hexasort problem in two variants:</text>
<text matrix="1 0 0 1 0 384" pin="h" transformations="translations" pos="0 41.6974" stroke="black" type="minipage" width="920" height="102.962" depth="89.208" valign="top"> \hl{Fitting Hexasort}\\
\textbf{Input:} A Hexasort instance $(G, S, t)$ consisting of
\begin{itemize}
\item an undirected graph $G = (V,E)$ = board
\item a sequence $S$ of monochromatic stacks $s=(c,h)$ of color $c$ and height $h$
\item a threshold $t \in \mathbb{N^+}$
\end{itemize}
\textbf{Task:} Can we place all stacks without running out of board space?</text>
<text matrix="1 0 0 1 0 172" pin="h" transformations="translations" pos="0 41.6974" stroke="black" type="minipage" width="920" height="69.5268" depth="55.692" valign="top"> \hl{Empty Hexasort}\\
\textbf{Input:} A Hexasort instance $(G, S, t)$\\
\textbf{Task:} Can we place all stacks without running out of board space and end with an empty board?</text>
<text layer="gamma" matrix="1 0 0 1 0 -96" pin="h" transformations="translations" pos="0 166.2" stroke="black" type="minipage" width="920" height="33.3872" depth="19.46" valign="top" style="default">No complications such as non-empty starting board or stacks with multiple colors are considered.</text>
</page>
