<text layer="Strong-NP" matrix="1 0 0 1 0 24.3898" pin="h" transformations="translations" pos="0 317.307" stroke="black" type="minipage" width="920" height="19.3928" depth="5.404" valign="top">\begin{itemize}
\item strong NP-hardness on disjoint spider graphs
\end{itemize}</text>
<text layer="Recap-Weak-NP" matrix="1 0 0 1 0 -192" pin="h" transformations="translations" pos="0 524" stroke="black" type="minipage" width="920" height="66.8192" depth="52.976" valign="top"> \hl{Recap on Weak NP-hardness}:\\
Weak NP-hardness describes a set of problems which are NP-hard in general, but are in P if every number in the input is polynomially bounded by the input size.</text>
<text matrix="1 0 0 1 0 -64" pin="h" transformations="translations" pos="0 244.205" stroke="black" type="minipage" width="920" height="52.8248" depth="38.92" valign="top">\textbf{Intuitive Understanding of Weak NP-hardness}: The problem is only hard when the input contains exponentially large numbers, if the numbers are small the problem is easy.</text>
<group layer="Weak-NP" matrix="1 0 0 1 22.7301 125.46">
